#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096562 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "c946c1c9-f3d2-5d9d-9390-8ee542ca15a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-745" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=e^{x}$ and let $P_2(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$. Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_2(x)$ is used to approximate $e^{1.1}$." :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_2$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial $P_2$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=1.1$. Plugging $x=1.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{3!} \\mid 1.1 - \\left.1\\right|^{3};\\left. \\mid R_{2} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{6} \\mid 0.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $M$, we need to compute $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,1.1]$. Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\nf^{(3)}(x) &= e^{x} \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}f^{(3)}(x) & &= & e^{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid f^{(3)}(x) &= e^x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis an increasing function on the interval $[1,1.1]$, its value is greatest at $x=1.1$. So\n\n$$\nM = \\mid f^{(3)}(1.1) \\mid =e^{1.1}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{e^{1.1}}{6}\\end{vmatrix}0.1\\right|^{3} \\approx 0.000\\,501\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096563 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "3f5e5945-cdaa-55b9-b360-9e68249480b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-746" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=\\ln(1-x)$ and let $P_3(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0$. Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when $P_3(x)$ is used to approximate $\\ln(0.9)$" :question/worked-solution "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error $R_3$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial $P_3$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_3 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{4!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.0\\right|^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(4)}(x) \\mid$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.1$. Plugging $x=0.1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\mid R_{3} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{4!} \\mid 0.1 - \\left.0\\right|^{4};\\left. \\mid R_{3} \\mid \\mid \\le \\frac{M}{24} \\mid 0.1\\right|^{4}\n$$\n\nTo find $M$, we need to compute $\\mid f^{(4)}(x) \\mid$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.1]$. Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= - (1)/((x - 1)^{2}) \\\\\nf^{‴}(x) &= (2)/((x - 1)^{3}) \\\\\nf^{(4)}(x) &= - (6)/((x - 1)^{4}) \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}f^{(4)}(x) & &= & - (6)/((x - 1)^{4})\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (6)/((x - 1)^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid f^{(4)}(x) &= \\dfrac{6}{(x-1)^4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis an increasing function on the interval $[0,0.1]$, its value is greatest at $x=0.1$. So\n\n$$\nM = \\mid f^{(4)}(0.1) \\mid =\\dfrac{6}{(0.1-1)^4} = \\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{vmatrix}R_3 & \\leq \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{6}{(0.9)^4} \\right)}{24}\\end{vmatrix}0.1\\right|^{4} \\approx 0.000\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b627a1f1-7038-57ab-bfe5-b97f4b51e653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{3} \\mid \\le 0.0043" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093855} {:answer/id #uuid "efd53f44-d783-53aa-b2ec-a65e9866aa1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{3} \\mid \\le 0.0032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093856} {:answer/id #uuid "fd88a69d-0f04-5249-81a7-2378df8b5b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{3} \\mid \\le 0.0002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093857} {:answer/id #uuid "134641d8-d62b-5b84-9026-efb75ed3b9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{3} \\mid \\le 0.0017" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093858} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc385c7-474c-5cbc-b16e-9c8a45d2714e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{3} \\mid \\le 0.0024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093859}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd32982-99ac-5951-be9c-a7d6abcdb3a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093854}] :question/id #uuid "2f0c0766-f92b-5029-93d1-2aeb7633b5b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-11585" :question/problem "Let $f(x) = e^{x}$ and let $P_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x = 1$. Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error $R_{3}$ obtained when $P_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $e^{0.65}$. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "decbd5ef-e64b-5cfb-8888-0027cc73993e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{3}{800}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095827} {:answer/id #uuid "f5018280-f2bd-5e10-82a9-5edd58c167cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{3}{11000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095828} {:answer/id #uuid "0cbb753a-a19e-521d-8436-94871efc85eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{9}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095829} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab177ad-8935-567c-a148-c29f4ea2662d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{9}{8000}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095830} {:answer/id #uuid "6a28873b-6ca0-525d-9aff-2a156fd2ac14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\mid R_{2} \\mid \\le \\frac{27}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095831}] :answer-field/id #uuid "93fc0622-6b89-5e60-84fd-c344c741bb85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095826}] :question/id #uuid "7bfbcc6e-0a0e-51f6-8574-b8c524edf820" :question/math-academy-id "q-51342" :question/problem "Let $f(x) = \\ln x$ and let $P_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x = 2$. Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error $R_{2}$ obtained when $P_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\ln 2.3$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
